,"From Middle High German grob, grop, from Old High German grob, girob, of uncertain origin. Compare also Low German groff, Dutch grof, Saterland Frisian groaf, and English gruff.\nPer Kroonen, from Proto-West Germanic *grob, from Proto-Germanic *grubaz (“coarse”), from Proto-Indo-European *gʰrewbʰ- (“to grind”). If so, then related to Old High German griobo (“twigs, kindling, firewood”), from Proto-Germanic *greubô (“greaves”). Also related to Proto-Slavic *grǫbъ.\nAlternatively, perhaps from Proto-West Germanic *gahrob, from Proto-Germanic *gahrubaz, *hrubaz (“scabby, crusty, rough”), from Proto-Indo-European *krewp- (“scab”). If so, cognate with Old High German riob (“scabby, leprous, mangy”), Old English hrēof (“rough, scabbed, leprous”)"]],"senses":[[16769,"grob","adj","coarse, rough",0],[16770,"grob","adj","uncouth, rude, crude, ill-defined",1]],"related_forms":[[25315,"grob","grobian","derived"],[25316,"grob","grobschmied","derived"]]},"harntreibendes":{"entries":[["harntreibendes",null,null,null]],"senses":[[223857,"harntreibendes","adj","strong/mixed nominative/accusative neuter singular of harntreibend",0]],"inflections":[["harntreibendes","harntreibend","neuter,nominative,singular,strong,without-article"]]},"kreuzspinne":{"entries":[["kreuzspinne",null,null,"Kreuz (“cross”) + Spinne (“spider”)",{"g":"f","decl":"||||n|n|n|n"}]],"senses":[[631863,"kreuzspinne","noun","Any spider of the genus Araneus.",0,"f"]]},"tautropfen":{"entries":[["tautropfen",null,"/ˈtaʊ̯ˌtʁɔp͡fn̩/","Tau + Tropfen",{"g":"m","decl":"|||s||||"}]],"senses":[[590347,"tautropfen","noun","dewdrop",0,"m"]]},"vorhand":{"entries":[["vorhand",null,"/ˈfoːɐ̯hant/","vor- + Hand",{"g":"f","decl":"||||3ände|3ände|3änden|3ände"}]],"senses":[[602858,"vorhand","noun","hand that goes first",0,"f",null,null,null,null,["card-games"]],[602859,"vorhand","noun","forehand",1,"f",null,null,null,null,["sports"]],[602860,"vorhand","noun","advantageous position",2,"f",["figuratively"]],[602861,"vorhand","noun","p